    {
      "id": "far-cf-deposit-expense-receivable-c3",
      "heading": "Transaction 6: an invoice with no cash still counts",
      "explanation_md": "Circle Films **films a dance competition, leaving a ₹3,500 invoice** with the customer.\n\nIt is tempting to say no cash means no accounting transaction. But **not all accounting transactions affect cash.** By completing the job and leaving an invoice, Circle Films now has a **receivable** from the customer, so assets increase. Because the receivable arises from providing a service, there is **additional revenue and more equity.**\n\nThis is very similar to transaction 3. The only difference is the **type of asset that increases**: accounts receivable instead of cash.\n\nTable: Accounts Receivable ₹0 + ₹3,500; Retained Earnings ₹1,250 + ₹3,500 = ₹4,750. Total assets ₹21,750 = Liabilities ₹2,000 + Equity ₹19,750.",
      "example_ids": [
        "far-ex-t6-dance"
      ],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Receivable",
          "meaning": "An amount a customer owes after a service is performed but not yet paid for; an asset.",
          "source_ref": "far--practice-circle-films-transaction-analysis#2"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "A credit sale of services raises accounts receivable and retained earnings; no cash moves.",
      "clarification": null,
      "check_questions": [
