      "type": "long",
      "stem": "Explain how liabilities, expenses and receivables enter the accounting equation using Circle Films' transactions 4, 5 and 6.",
      "options": [],
      "answer": "",
      "accept": [],
      "explanation": "",
      "model_answer_md": "**Liability (transaction 4).** A ₹2,000 deposit is received before the filming. Cash rises but the company owes the service, so liabilities rise (Unearned Revenue).\n\n**Expense (transaction 5).** ₹250 is paid for an ad. A decrease in assets from operating the business is an expense; it lowers net income and retained earnings.\n\n**Receivable (transaction 6).** A ₹3,500 invoice is left with the customer. No cash arrives, yet the firm now has a receivable (an asset) and additional revenue, so equity rises.\n\nIn all three the equation stays in balance because each change on one side is matched on the other.",
      "rubric": [
        {
          "point": "Deposit creates a liability",
